[{"record_id":"rec_000253","title":"Coding agents author interpretable single-cell embedding models from the literature","abstract":"The single-cell literature catalogs cell states as validated marker-gene programs — a sparse, compositional prior. Conventional embedding methods do not leverage this prior and learn cell-state structure de novo from the expression matrix, producing dense dimensions needing post-hoc interpretation and batch correction. Here we show coding agents can author single-cell embedding models directly from the literature. Given a scenario that focuses this literature lens on a chosen biological subdomain, the agent edits a structured Python template, curating named, literature-cited gene programs and composing them into axes, without a gene-set database, training, or sight of the data. Across mouse and human tissues these zero-shot embeddings are competitive in biological quality with conventional, foundation-model, and program-informed baselines, batch-robust by construction and reproducible across runs, complementing data-driven embeddings. Because each dimension is a named, cited gene program, the embedding is interpretable and auditable, and its composable axes can be steered into a developmental tree.","doi":"10.64898/2026.07.07.737048","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000253","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract says coding agents \"author single-cell embedding models directly from the literature\" by editing a Python template, which is a wrapper/workflow around model construction rather than a new primary model. The model is driven by gene programs, not a substantive text-bio generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"coding agents can author single-cell embedding models directly from the literature\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000253","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work authors interpretable single-cell embedding models; embeddings are not a generative architecture. It compares against foundation-model baselines rather than presenting a foundation model itself.","evidence_snippet":"\"author interpretable single-cell embedding models ... zero-shot embeddings\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000257","title":"Dissecting and directing pathology foundation models","abstract":"Foundation models (FMs) are central to digital pathology, encoding histology images into dense embeddings for facilitating diagnostic classification, molecular alteration prediction, and clinical outcome modeling. However, the opacity of these embeddings renders FM-based systems “black boxes,” limiting their trustworthiness for clinical translation and utility for scientific discovery. Here, we introduce PICASSO (Pathology Image Concept Atlas built via SparSe dictiOnary learning), a framework that makes pathology FMs interpretable and controllable. PICASSO decomposes FM embeddings into human-interpretable visual concepts using a sparse autoencoder. It is trained on more than 120 million tissue patches across 32 cancer types, producing the first pan-cancer atlas of histomorphological concepts. We demonstrate that PICASSO enables diverse downstream applications of FM embeddings by exposing interpretable structure within learned representations and supporting concept-level intervention. It enables auditing of clinical model behavior by revealing the morphological features driving predictions. Beyond transparency and validation, PICASSO enables the discovery of new biological insights; for example, it identified hobnailing epithelial morphology as a previously unrecognized biomarker of EGFR mutations in lung adenocarcinoma. By linking PICASSO-derived concepts with spatial transcriptomics, we uncover associations between morphological patterns and gene expression programs. Furthermore, PICASSO allows suppression of concepts associated with technical artifacts, thereby reducing model reliance on spurious signals. Finally, PICASSO enables controlled manipulation of learned concepts to generate counterfactual embeddings for exploratory therapeutic analysis, such as modulating tumour-infiltrating lymphocyte density to assess impacts on predict survival outcomes. Together, PICASSO provides a principled framework for transforming pathology FMs into platforms for mechanistic insight and discovery.","doi":"10.64898/2026.06.12.731496","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000257","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Pathology foundation model work on histology images and embeddings. No substantive text/language component is described.","evidence_snippet":"“foundation models (FMs) are central to digital pathology, encoding histology images into dense embeddings”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000257","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"PICASSO is a framework that interprets and controls existing pathology foundation models rather than introducing a new generative architecture. The abstract does describe large-scale pretraining on 120 million tissue patches for pathology FMs.","evidence_snippet":"“makes pathology FMs interpretable and controllable” and “trained on more than 120 million tissue patches”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000259","title":"Toward generalizable and interpretable AI in regulatory genomics","abstract":"… of cell-type-specific regulatory activity, including chromatin accessibility, TF binding, histone modifications, transcription initiation and steady-state gene expression… A foundation model of …","doi":"10.1038/s41588-026-02670-3","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000259","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The fragment refers to regulatory genomics and a foundation model, but the record does not clearly show a primary model paper versus review/commentary. No substantive text component is evident.","evidence_snippet":"“Toward generalizable and interpretable AI in regulatory genomics” and “A foundation model of …”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000259","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record is too fragmentary to determine whether this is a primary model paper or a r