dation model trained …”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000264","title":"CAUSALPERT: GROUNDING LLM HYPOTHESES IN REGULATORY NETWORKS FOR GENE PERTURBATION PREDICTION","abstract":"… Problem setup for predicting gene expression vectors of … the limits of LLM reasoning within transcriptomic space. We … baseline relies on a Large Language Model (LLM) to retrieve a …","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000264","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract fragment show an LLM being used to generate hypotheses that are grounded in regulatory networks for gene perturbation prediction. That supports a substantive text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“GROUNDING LLM HYPOTHESES IN REGULATORY NETWORKS FOR GENE PERTURBATION PREDICTION”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000264","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"CAUSALPERT is described as grounding LLM hypotheses in regulatory networks for perturbation prediction, which is an application workflow around an LLM. The abstract does not describe a genuinely generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“GROUNDING LLM HYPOTHESES IN REGULATORY NETWORKS FOR GENE PERTURBATION PREDICTION”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000265","title":"Findings from Sparse Autoencoders for DNA Sequence Models: Motif Detectors, Reading-Frame Features, and the Scarcity of Regulatory Logic","abstract":"Abstract\n                Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) have become the standard tool for decomposing the internal activations of language models into human-interpretable features, but their behaviour on DNA sequence models— transformers and long-convolution models pretrained on genomes—remains largely uncharted. Rather than train an exhaustive suite, we present a deliberately narrow study: we fit top-𝐾 SAEs to the residual stream at a sweep of layers of Nucleotide Transformer v2 (500M) and HyenaDNA, and dissect the resulting features against a curated panel of genomic annotations (GENCODE splice sites and reading frames, ENCODE transcription-factor peaks, CpG islands, promoter elements, and repeats). Four findings emerge. First, SAE features are far more monosemantic than raw neurons: at the most interpretable layer, 62% of alive SAE features receive a confident label versus 18% of neurons, and the fraction aligning with a genomic annotation at AUROC&gt;0.8 rises from 5% to 21%. Second, interpretability is strongly layer-dependent, peaking near 0.6 relative depth and collapsing in the final layers. Third, the recovered vocabulary is dominated by local sequence grammar—single-motif detectors (splice donor GT, start codon ATG, poly-A signals, TATA boxes, individual TF motifs), GC/CpG composition, and reading-frame period-3 features that fire selectively in coding sequence. Fourth, and in contrast, features encoding regulatory logic—combinatorial or context-conditional computation not reducible to a single motif—are rare (1.4% of features by a conditional-selectivity test) and weak. Cross-model matching is high for simple motifs and near chance for combinatorial features, and causal ablation confirms that the interpretable features are genuinely used downstream. These results mirror recent single-cell findings that foundation models encode organized biological knowledge but little regulatory logic, and suggest that SAEs on current DNA models recover a genomic dictionary more than a genomic grammar engine. Code and trained SAEs are released.","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-10434163/v1","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000265","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Original study of sparse autoencoders on DNA sequence models pretrained on genomes. The abstract describes genomic sequence features, not a substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"DNA sequence models ... pretrained on genomes\"; \"fit top-K SAEs\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000265","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is an interpretability study of existing DNA sequence models, not a new generative model paper. The abstract mentions pretrained genome models but does not clearly establish foundation-model-like scope or generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"we fit top-K SAEs ... of Nucleotide Transformer v2 (500M) and HyenaDNA\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no